course: dictionary
lesson: 7993
title: Recycle: use it again
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To recycle means to collect something after we have used it, so it can be broken down and made into something new. «An aluminium can can be recycled and be back on the shelf within about two months.» The clue is "back on the shelf": the can comes back as a new can.
explain: To recycle can also mean to use something again, instead of throwing it away. Old clothes can be passed on or sold second-hand, so they are used again. Recycle is a verb, so it changes: recycles, recycled, recycling.
explain: You can also recycle an idea or a joke: you use it again, often when it is no longer fresh. The words around the word tell you which meaning fits. Cans and glass point to the first meaning. An old joke told again points to the third.
fact: To recycle means to collect used things so they can be made into something new.
fact: «Aluminium» and glass can be recycled again and again without wearing out.
fact: Recycling metals uses much less energy than mining new ore.
fact: Paper, cardboard, glass, metal cans and some plastics go in the recycling bin.
fact: Many kinds of plastic are hard to recycle, so only a small share of all plastic ever made has been recycled.
exercise: What does recycle mean in «Paper and glass go in the recycling bin»? | To paint something bright | *To collect used things so they can be made into something new | To throw something away in the rubbish | To buy something brand new
exercise: Mum says, «Don't throw that old jar away, recycle it as a pencil pot.» What does recycle mean here? | To break it into tiny pieces | *To use it again for a new purpose | To throw it away | To sell it to a shop for money
exercise: Copy the word from «Aluminium and glass can be recycled again and again without wearing out» that means many times over. | *again | wearing | glass | Aluminium
test: Jamie says, «Old jokes are not fun, but he keeps recycling them.» What does recycling mean there? | To collect them for the recycling centre | To write them down in a book | To put them in the bin | *To use them again, even though they are no longer fresh
test: Which of these goes in the recycling bin? | Old wet sponge | Half-eaten apple | *Empty metal can | Used tissue
exam: Which sentence uses recycle to mean using something again instead of throwing it away? | Ella bought a brand-new box from the shop | *Ella turned the old box into a den instead of binning it | Ella put the old box straight in the bin | Ella drew a picture of a box
